# Reads a command-line interface described as a [usage](https://usage.jdx.dev) spec.
#
# A usage spec describes a program's commands, flags, and arguments once, in KDL, and usage generates its reference,
# man pages, and shell completions from it. A Ruby program that parses its own command line by hand drifts from that
# description: a flag added to the spec reaches the docs and not the parser, or the reverse.
#
# This module reads the JSON that `usage generate json` writes. It aims at the whole of usage's spec, and reads only
# what it acts on: a spec that uses anything else raises `Usage::SpecError` when it is read, naming what it uses.
#
# Use it to give a Ruby program the interface its usage spec describes.
#
# ## Examples
#
# ```ruby
# require "usage"
# spec = Usage::Spec.read(<<~JSON)
#   {"bin": "todo", "about": "Keep a list of things to do",
#    "cmd": {"subcommands": {"add": {"name": "add", "full_cmd": ["add"], "help": "Add an item",
#                                    "args": [{"name": "text", "required": true}]}}}}
# JSON
# spec.commands.find(["add"]).args.map(&:to_s) # => ["<text>"]
# ```
module Usage
  # The version of the gem, `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`.
  VERSION: String

  # The version of usage whose specs the gem reads, such as `"7.4.1"`. A spec whose `min_usage_version` is newer is
  # refused, because a newer usage may write what the gem has never seen.
  USAGE_VERSION: String

  # A value in the JSON that `usage generate json` writes.
  type json = String | Integer | Float | bool | nil | Array[json] | Hash[Symbol, json]

  # One object of that JSON, such as a command or a flag, with its keys as Symbols.
  type object = Hash[Symbol, json]

  # The keys of one kind of object that the gem accepts: a key it reads, such as `help`, or a key with the one value
  # it accepts, written as the key, `=`, and the value as JSON, such as `hide=false`.
  type keys = Array[String]
end
